-- Magna Carta (1215), English translation in McKechnie 1905: THE NOUNS MODULE.
-- Types only: the parties the Charter names, the acts it speaks of, the answers the
-- rules give, and the small kinds the chapters sort things into.  No rules.
-- Every other mc15- module imports this one.  Names are global to the whole encoding
-- (a name defined in two imported modules cannot be used), so every rule elsewhere
-- starts with its chapter, as in `ch 9 -- ...`.
-- GENERATED from tools/templates/mc15-nouns.l4.in by tools/build.py; edit the template.

§ `The nouns`

§§ `The answers the rules give`

-- THE THREE ANSWER TYPES.  Each has a positive value, a "no" value and a "not covered" value.
--
-- The "no" value (`Not barred`, `Not entitled`, `Not obliged`) is given ONLY where the text itself
-- says no in its own words: a condition it introduces with "if", "only if", "as long as" (also
-- "so long as", "while", "until") or "unless" (also "except", "save", "provided that"); a limit it
-- closes with "only", "nothing but", "no other ... than" or a ceiling ("not more than"); or a
-- definition that says "means".  Every branch that answers "no" carries a `-- FALSE-kind` comment
-- that quotes those words.  A "no" is also a claim about one chapter only: a function that can
-- answer it is named for its chapter ("Under chapter 9, ...") and says nothing about any other law.
--
-- The "not covered" value (`Bar not covered`, `Entitlement not covered`, `Obligation not covered`)
-- is given everywhere else: where the text is silent, where it lists things without closing the
-- list, where it says "includes", where a term is left undefined, or where it points to a power or
-- authority outside the text.  Every such branch carries a `-- NOTHING-kind` comment that says which.
-- A "not covered" value is the encoding's NOTHING; it is asserted, never left to chance.

-- "May the Crown or an officer do this to me?"  Barred: the text's own words bar the act on the
-- facts given.
DECLARE Bar IS ONE OF
    Barred
    `Not barred`
    `Bar not covered`

-- "Am I entitled to this?"
DECLARE Entitlement IS ONE OF
    Entitled
    `Not entitled`
    `Entitlement not covered`

-- "Must I (or must someone) do this?"
DECLARE Obligation IS ONE OF
    Obliged
    `Not obliged`
    `Obligation not covered`

-- Money in the units the text uses.  The text gives no rate between pounds and shillings,
-- so the two are never added or compared with each other.
DECLARE Sterling IS ONE OF
    Pounds HAS amount IS A NUMBER
    Shillings HAS amount IS A NUMBER

§§ `Parties`

-- The parties that can be the PARTY of a regulative rule.  "We" and "our" are the King;
-- "our heirs" are not a separate party here (fork F1).
DECLARE Party IS ONE OF
    `the King`
    `a bailiff`
    `a sheriff`
    `a constable`
    `a guardian`
    `a lord of the fee`
    `a surety`
    `a coroner`
    `the King's chief justiciar`
    `the nearest kinsfolk and friends of the deceased`
    `a Welshman`
    `a member of the kingdom, clergy or lay`
    `the barons`
    `the five-and-twenty barons`
    `four of the five-and-twenty barons`
    `the barons who are left of the five-and-twenty`
    `the baron chosen in the place of one of the five-and-twenty`
    `whoever in the country desires it`
    `any other person`

§§ `Acts`

DECLARE Act IS ONE OF
    `infringe the freedom of the English church, her rights or her liberties`
    `interfere with the freedom of elections of the English church`
    `demand relief or a fine from an heir who was a minor in wardship`
    `take from the heir's land more than reasonable produce, reasonable customs and reasonable services`
    `make destruction or waste of the men or goods of the wardship`
    `keep up the houses, parks, places for live-stock, fishponds, mills and other things pertaining to the land`
    `restore to the heir all his land, stocked with ploughs and implements of husbandry`
    `seize land or rent for a debt`
    `answer for the debt of the principal debtor`
    `have the lands and rents of the debtor until indemnified`
    `take from the debt anything except the principal sum contained in the bond`
    `cause the archbishops, bishops, abbots, earls and greater barons to be summoned by letters under seal`
    `cause all others who hold of the King in chief to be summoned generally through the sheriffs and bailiffs, for a fixed date after at least forty days and at a fixed place`
    `specify the reason of the summons in all letters of summons`
    `grant a licence to take an aid from his own free tenants`
    `send two justiciars through every county four times a year`
    `hold pleas of the Crown`
    `distribute the chattels of the freeman who died intestate`
    `take corn or other provisions without immediately tendering money`
    `compel a knight to give money in lieu of castle-guard`
    `take the horses or carts of a freeman for transport duty against his will`
    `take wood which is not the King's for the King's work, against the will of the owner`
    `retain the lands of a convicted felon beyond one year and one day`
    `attach and catalogue chattels of the deceased found upon the lay fief, to the value of the debt, at the sight of lawful men`
    `put a man to his law upon his own mere word of mouth, without credible witnesses`
    `have wardship of an heir or land by reason of a fee-farm, socage, burgage or petty serjeanty`
    `set forth against a freeman, or send against him, otherwise than by the lawful judgment of his peers and by the law of the land`
    `sell right or justice`
    `refuse right or justice`
    `delay right or justice`
    `take from the heir of a holder of an escheat a relief or service greater than the baron would have had`
    `hold the escheat in the same manner in which the baron held it`
    `appoint as a justice, constable, sheriff or bailiff one who does not know the law of the realm and mean to observe it well`
    `restore the hostages and charters delivered to him by Englishmen as sureties of the peace or of faithful service`
    `remove from their bailiwicks the relations of Gerard de Athyes named in chapter 50`
    `banish from the kingdom all foreign-born knights, cross-bowmen, serjeants and mercenary soldiers who have come with horses and arms to the kingdom's hurt`
    `restore the lands, castles, franchises or right from which a man was dispossessed or removed without the legal judgment of his peers`
    `grant full justice concerning the possessions for which he had respite`
    `grant full justice to all who complain of the matters in chapter 53`
    `restore to Welshmen what he disseised or removed without the legal judgment of their peers`
    `restore to the King and his men what a Welshman disseised or removed without the legal judgment of their peers`
    `grant full justice in accordance with the laws of the Welsh concerning the possessions taken from Welshmen for which he had respite`
    `give up the son of Llywelyn, the hostages of Wales and the charters delivered as security for the peace`
    `deal with Alexander, King of Scots, in the same manner as with the other barons of England`
    `observe towards his men the customs and liberties which the King has granted towards his men`
    `choose five-and-twenty barons of the kingdom, whomsoever they will`
    `observe and hold, and cause to be observed, the peace and liberties granted and confirmed by the Charter`
    `repair to the King, or to his justiciar if he is out of the realm, and, laying the transgression before him, petition to have it corrected without delay`
    `refer the matter to the rest of the five-and-twenty barons`
    `distrain and distress the King in all possible ways, together with the community of the whole land, by seizing his castles, lands and possessions and in any other way they can`
    `harm the person of the King, of the queen or of their children in distraining and distressing the King`
    `resume their old relations towards the King`
    `swear to obey the orders of the five-and-twenty barons for the execution of the aforesaid matters and, along with them, molest the King to the utmost of his power`
    `forbid anyone to swear to obey the orders of the five-and-twenty barons`
    `compel by his command those in the land who of themselves and of their own accord are unwilling to swear to the twenty-five, to swear to that effect`
    `choose another baron in his place according to their own judgment`
    `be sworn in the same way as the others`
    `swear that they will faithfully observe all that is aforesaid, and cause it to be observed with all their might`
    `procure from anyone, directly or indirectly, anything whereby any part of these concessions and liberties might be revoked or diminished`
    `use personally or by another a thing procured to revoke or diminish a part of these concessions and liberties`
    `keep all the conditions of the Charter in good faith and without evil intent`

§§ `Kinds`

-- Chapter 2: the three holdings the text prices.
DECLARE HoldingKind IS ONE OF
    `a whole earl's barony`
    `a whole barony`
    `a whole knight's fee`

-- Chapter 4: how the guardian came to hold the wardship.
DECLARE GuardianKind IS ONE OF
    `committed by the King to the sheriff or another accountable to him`
    `given or sold by the King to someone`

-- Chapter 4: what follows if the guardian lays waste.
DECLARE WasteConsequence IS ONE OF
    `The King takes amends and the land is committed to two lawful and discreet men of that fee`
    `The wardship is lost and transferred to two lawful and discreet men of that fief`
    `Nothing follows under chapter 4`

-- Chapter 7: what a widow may be asked to give.
DECLARE Demand IS ONE OF
    `something for her dower`
    `something for her marriage portion`
    `something for the inheritance which her husband and she held on the day of his death`
    `some other payment`

-- Chapter 8: whose consent a widow's security is against.
DECLARE ConsentOf IS ONE OF
    `the King's consent`
    `the consent of the lord of whom she holds`

-- Chapters 12 to 15: scutage and aid.
DECLARE LevyKind IS ONE OF
    Scutage
    Aid

-- Chapters 12 and 15: the three occasions the text names for an aid.  The owner is the King
-- in chapter 12 and the lord in chapter 15, so the names carry no owner.
DECLARE AidPurpose IS ONE OF
    `ransoming the person`
    `making the eldest son a knight`
    `once marrying the eldest daughter`
    `some other purpose`

-- Chapter 18: the three inquests the text names.
DECLARE InquestKind IS ONE OF
    `novel disseisin`
    `mort d'ancestor`
    `darrein presentment`
    `some other kind of inquest`

-- Chapter 20: who is amerced.
DECLARE ClassOfPerson IS ONE OF
    `a freeman`
    `a merchant`
    `a villein`

-- Chapter 20: what is saved to him when he is amerced.
DECLARE SavedItem IS ONE OF
    `his contenement`
    `his wares`
    `his wainage`

-- Chapter 25: the units of local government the text names.
DECLARE UnitOfRent IS ONE OF
    `a county`
    `a hundred`
    `a wapentake`
    `a trithing`
    `a demesne manor`
    `some other unit`

-- Chapter 27: how the chattels of a freeman who died intestate are distributed.
DECLARE Distribution IS ONE OF
    `By the hands of his nearest kinsfolk and friends, under the supervision of the church`
    `Distribution not covered`

-- Chapter 35: what the text sells by a single standard.
DECLARE Commodity IS ONE OF
    `wine`
    `ale`
    `corn`
    `cloth, whether dyed, or russet, or halberget`
    `a weight`
    `something else sold`

-- Chapter 35: the standard the text names.  For wine, ale and weights it says "one", and gives no size.
DECLARE Standard IS ONE OF
    `one measure, whose size the text does not state`
    `the London quarter`
    `two ells within the selvedges`
    `one weight, whose size the text does not state`
    `Standard not covered`

-- Chapter 37: the tenure by reason of which the King is said not to take wardship.
DECLARE TenureOfKing IS ONE OF
    `fee-farm`
    `socage`
    `burgage`
    `petty serjeanty held of the King by rendering knives, arrows or the like`
    `some other tenure`

-- Chapter 37: what the wardship would be of.
DECLARE WardshipOf IS ONE OF
    `the heir`
    `land of his held of the fief of the other lord by knight's service`
    `the fee-farm, socage or burgage itself`

-- Chapter 40: the three dealings in right or justice the text rules out.
DECLARE JusticeDealing IS ONE OF
    `to sell`
    `to refuse`
    `to delay`

-- Chapter 45: the offices the text names.
DECLARE OfficeKind IS ONE OF
    `a justice`
    `a constable`
    `a sheriff`
    `a bailiff`
    `some other office`

-- Chapters 52, 55, 56 and 59: who decides.
DECLARE Forum IS ONE OF
    `the five-and-twenty barons`
    `the five-and-twenty barons, or the majority of them, along with the archbishop of Canterbury and such others as he brings`
    `the five-and-twenty barons, or the majority of them, without the archbishop of Canterbury`
    `the judgment of the Welshman's peers in the marches`
    `the judgment of his peers in the King's court`
    `Forum not covered`

-- Chapter 55: what is done with an unjust fine or amercement.
DECLARE Remedy IS ONE OF
    `Remitted entirely, or else dealt with by the five-and-twenty barons`
    `Remedy not covered`

